# Origin used in ticket emails. sendTicketEmails embeds QR images from
# {PUBLIC_ORIGIN}/api/ticket-qr, and serveTicketQr encodes a claim URL
# ({PUBLIC_ORIGIN}/claim?ticket=X&event=Y) into each one.
# Its host, less any leading "www.", is also the domain all mail is sent
# from (<prefix>@domain, prefix set in Site Config, no-reply by default), so
# it must be the domain verified on the Resend account.
# Defaults to https://sekaibeyond.com in code. Forks MUST override this or
# their tickets will point at the original deployment. Don't blank it
# (PUBLIC_ORIGIN=): serveTicketQr then answers 500 and the emails carry
# relative image URLs that no mail client can load.
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-site.example.com

# The OAuth client ID of the site's Sign in with Google button, the same value
# the site is built with as VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. A Google sign-in is refused
# unless its token was issued for this client. The deploy workflow fills it in
# from that secret, so it only needs setting here for a deploy from your machine.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# NOTE: the Resend API key is NOT set here. It is a secret managed by
# Google Secret Manager (defineSecret in resendClient.ts). Set it with:
#   firebase functions:secrets:set RESEND_API_KEY

# Optional. Per-day cap on outgoing emails (matches the Resend plan tier).
# Enforced server-side against the Resend usage cached in system/resendQuota.
# Default: 100 (Resend free plan).
# RESEND_DAILY_CAP=100

# Optional. How many of those are held back from ticket and passport mail, so a
# big send can't stop someone getting a sign-up or password code. At most half
# the cap. Default: 20.
# ACCOUNT_MAIL_RESERVE=20

# NOTE: the secret key mixed into password hashes and email codes is a secret
# too, and must exist before functions deploy. Changing it later invalidates
# every password, so set it once, to a long random string:
#   firebase functions:secrets:set PASSWORD_PEPPER

# NOTE: not set here either, but every sign-in fails without it. The functions
# answer each sign-in with a Firebase custom token, which a deployed function
# has signed by Google's IAM as the service account it runs as (the default
# compute one, PROJECT_NUMBER-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com). That
# account needs the Service Account Token Creator role on itself, and the IAM
# Service Account Credentials API (iamcredentials.googleapis.com) must be on.
# Without them every sign-in answers 500, and the function's log says
# "Permission 'iam.serviceAccounts.signBlob' denied". The emulator signs tokens
# itself, so it works there regardless. Grant the role once, with:
#   gcloud iam service-accounts add-iam-policy-binding PROJECT_NUMBER-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com --member="serviceAccount:PROJECT_NUMBER-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com" --role="roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator" --project=PROJECT_ID

# Wallet passes: each ticket in a ticket email has an Add to Wallet button
# ({PUBLIC_ORIGIN}/api/wallet-pass), whose first tap makes an Apple/Google
# Wallet pass with WalletWallet (walletwallet.dev). The API key is a secret,
# like Resend's, and must exist before functions deploy. Set it with:
#   firebase functions:secrets:set WALLETWALLET_API_KEY
# Without one (or in the emulator) the button says passes are unavailable.

# Optional. Wallet passes made per calendar month (UTC), after which the button
# says so until the 1st. WalletWallet's plan limit applies too, whichever is
# lower. 0 turns passes off and drops the button from ticket emails.
# Default: 1000 (WalletWallet free plan).
# WALLET_PASS_MONTHLY_CAP=1000

# Optional. New wallet passes one ticket can make per day (site time zone).
# Tapping the button again, even several times at once, reuses the ticket's
# pass, so this only counts when there's none to reuse: after a void and
# unvoid, or a retry after WalletWallet didn't answer. 0 turns passes off.
# Default: 2.
# WALLET_PASS_DAILY_CAP_PER_TICKET=2

# Optional. The issuer a wallet pass names. Default: Sekai Beyond.
# WALLET_PASS_ORGANIZATION=Sekai Beyond

# Optional. Max attendees processed per sendTicketEmails invocation, also the
# per-call Resend batch size (Resend caps batches at 100). The client loops
# with cursor/drain pagination. Default: 100.
# SEND_CHUNK_SIZE=100

# Optional. Max emails waiting in the scheduledMail overflow queue. Once it is
# full, sends past the daily cap are rejected instead of queued. Default: 500.
# RESEND_QUEUE_CAP=500

# Optional. Max attendee rows per importEventAttendees call. Default: 1000.
# IMPORT_MAX_ROWS=1000

# Optional. Max size of an uploaded image (avatars, banners, admin images) in
# megabytes. The web app reads it from getUploadLimits. Default: 10.
# MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE_MB=10

# Optional. Max size of the con hero video in megabytes. Keep it well under 24:
# the file travels as base64 (4/3 its size) in a request Cloud Run caps at 32MiB.
# The web app reads it from getUploadLimits. Default: 20.
# MAX_VIDEO_UPLOAD_SIZE_MB=20

# Recommended. Salt for the hashed scan-client keys behind the QR and passport
# scan quotas. A fallback is in source, so without your own value someone with
# both the source and a database export could brute-force IPv4 addresses back
# out of the hashes. Any long random string works.
# SCAN_CLIENT_SALT=
